  "pepTitle": "Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) -Given for people exposed to HIV.",
  "pepDesc": "PEP stands for post-exposure prophylaxis. The word “prophylaxis” means to prevent or control the spread of an infection or disease. PEP means taking HIV medicines within 72 hours (3 days) after a possible exposure to HIV to prevent HIV.",
  "condomStepsTitle": "How to use a condom in 10 steps- for age greater than 18",
  "condomStepsSubtitle": "Follow the 10 steps below to avoid condom breakage or slippage. The how to put on a condom video shows these steps.",
  "condomStep1": "Check the expiry date. Do not use expired condoms.",
  "condomStep2": "Press the package between your fingers. Do not use if there is no resistance from air inside.",
  "condomStep3": "Tear open the condom package from the corner and remove the condom. Do not use teeth or sharp objects to open as this can damage the condom.",
  "condomStep4": "Identify which way the condom unrolls.",
  "condomStep5": "Pinch the receptor tip of the condom to remove air.",
  "condomStep6": "Place the condom on the head of the penis while still pinching the condom receptor tip.",
  "condomStep7": "Unroll the condom to the base of the shaft.",
  "condomStep8": "Insert the penis into vagina.",
  "condomStep9": "Pull out while still erect, holding the base of the condom firmly.",
  "condomStep10": "Remove the condom from the penis and discard.",
  "hivTestingDesc": "HIV testing services refer to the full range of services that include HIV testing, counseling and linkage to appropriate HIV prevention, treatment and care. The following are HIV testing methods.",
  "pitcTitle": "Provider Initiated Testing and Counseling (PITC): ",
  "pitcDesc": "This refers to HIV testing, and counseling recommended by a provider during a visit to a health care facility.",
  "vctTitle": "Voluntary Counseling and Testing (VCT): ",
  "vctDesc": "VCT is initiated by clients seeking to know their HIV status.",
  "artDesc1": "People with HIV need to start taking special medicine called antiretroviral therapy, or ART, as soon as they can. This medicine helps slow down the HIV virus in their bodies. When someone takes ART, the amount of virus goes down, and their immune system gets stronger.",
  "artDesc2": "Everyone, including children, Adolescents and adults, who have HIV should begin this treatment right after they find out they have the virus. Starting treatment early can help people live longer, stay healthier, and reduce the chances of passing HIV to others. It also helps prevent other illnesses and makes life better for those living with HIV.",
